  "summary_en": "Philosophical article on human likeness in AI and robotics. It argues that strong-AI debates, the Turing Test and the ambiguity of 'autonomy' raise no serious ethical issues, but that deliberate anthropomorphic design in human-machine interaction is ethically significant because artefacts are designed to be treated like humans.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Strong AI debates are largely irrelevant to actual AI and robotics research practice.",
   "Technological and personal autonomy are ambiguous terms that cause unintentional anthropomorphism.",
   "Theoretical human likeness raises no serious ethical issues.",
   "Intentional anthropomorphic design in human-machine interaction is ethically significant.",
   "Ethical issues concern concrete artefacts and technology design."
